iOS: an atomic whose operand crosses a 16-byte boundary is done inline

An x86 `xchg [mem], reg` or `lock xadd/add/sub/and/or/xor` on a
misaligned operand is legal and atomic on x86 (a split lock). FEX lowers
each to one LSE instruction (SWPAL, LDADDAL, LDCLRAL, LDSETAL, LDEORAL).
Apple's cores (LSE2) run those on any operand inside one 16-byte block
and raise an alignment fault for one that crosses a 16-byte boundary;
Madeira's exception thread then does the operation ([mach_exc]
UNALIGNED-LSE, signal_arm64_ios.c), 25-30 us each with the faulting
thread waiting.

- Far Cry 4 (FC64.dll, ARM64EC module): about 450,000 at each start of
  FarCry4.exe (three per launch), about 54 s of the 93 s from launch to
  the splash screen, then about 5,000 a second in game.
- Ubisoft Connect (32-bit upc.exe, WOW64 module): about 200,000 at its
  start.

With FEX_IOS_HOST, AtomicSwap and AtomicFetch{Add,Sub,And,CLR,Or,Xor}
go through one emitter helper. For an operand of 2, 4 or 8 bytes it
first tests bit 4 of the first ^ last byte's address (add, eor, tbnz;
NZCV, which holds the guest flags, is not touched). Not crossing: the
original instruction, as before. Crossing: the first and the last byte
are probed with an atomic byte add of zero, so a fault (unmapped,
read-only or guard page) is raised at the access's own pages before
anything is held; a per-process spin lock is taken (full barrier after
it), the operand is loaded, computed and stored with plain accesses, the
lock is released (stlr, then a full barrier) and the old value goes to
the destination zero-extended, as the LSE instruction does. A lock not
had within 65,536 tries leaves the operation to the original instruction
and its trap. Crossing atomics stay serialised with each other, as the
exception thread serialised them, and are not atomic against other
threads' plain accesses, as before. A byte operand cannot cross and is
emitted as before, as are CAS, CASPair, AtomicFetchNeg and the LL/SC
paths (a crossing lock cmpxchg still traps). The lock's address is a
constant in the generated code, which is fine while FEX's code cache
(EnableCodeCachingWIP) stays off, as in Madeira.

On by default; MADEIRA_FEX_SPLIT_LOCK=0 when a program starts emits the
original instruction alone, byte for byte.

Applies on willfaust/FEX 1dc6ed4 (Madeira's FEX pin), both modules
(xtajit64.dll and xtajit.dll). Madeira-only: not for FEX-Emu.

